Rotating a PDF in Delphi 7 requires using an external library that can manipulate PDF files. Here's an example of how you can use the `iTextSharp` library to rotate a PDF in Delphi 7:

**Prerequisites:**

1. Download and install the `iTextSharp` library from [here](https://github.com/itext队/iTextSharp/tree/v5.5.13).
2. Add the `iTextSharp` DLL to your Delphi 7 project by creating a new directory in the `B aliases` section of the Project Options, and then add the DLL file to that directory.

**Delphi 7 Code:**
```pascal
unit RotatePDF;

interface

uses
  Windows, SysUtils, Classes, iTextSharp;

type
  TPDFRotator = class
  private
    FPDF: TPDF;
  public
    constructor Create(const PDFPath: string);
    procedure RotatePages(const pageIndex: Integer; const rotation: TPDFRotation);
    procedure Save(constdestinationPath: string);
  end;

implementation

constructor TPDFRotator.Create(const PDFPath: string);
begin
  FPDF := TPDF.New(PDFPath);
end;

procedure TPDFRotator.RotatePages(const pageIndex: Integer; const rotation: TPDFRotation);
var
  page: IPdfPage;
begin
  page := FPDF.GetPage(pageIndex);
  page.Rotate(rotation);
end;

procedure TPDFRotator.Save(const destinationPath: string);
begin
  FPDF.Save(destinationPath);
end;

end.
```
**Usage:**
```pascal
var
  rotator: TPDFRotator;

begin
  rotator := TPDFRotator.Create('input.pdf');
  rotator.RotatePages(1, pdoTRotateClockwise); // Rotate the second page clockwise
  rotator.Save('output.pdf');
  FreeAndNil(rotator);
end.
```
In this example, the `TPDFRotator` class creates a PDF object using the `IPdfPage` interface from `iTextSharp`. The `RotatePages` method rotates the specified page by the specified rotation (clockwise or counterclockwise). The `Save` method saves the modified PDF to a new file.

